<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Thomas Church, Leipzig</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thomaskirche</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St Thomas Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> is a late Gothic Lutheran church, home of the Thomanerchor boys' choir founded in 1212. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> was its cantor from 1723 until his death in 1750, and his remains have rested in the church since 1950. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> preached there in 1539, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was introduced in Leipzig.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-thomas-church-leipzig.jpg" alt="St. Thomas Church, Leipzig" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dirk Goldhahn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leipzig_Thomaskirche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The site held a church at least from the 12th century, and foundations of a Romanesque building have been found in the choir and crossing of the present church. Between 1212 and 1222 the earlier structure became the church of the new St. Thomas college of Augustinian canons, founded by Markgraf Dietrich von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. The college later became the core of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, founded in 1409.</p><p>In 1217 the minnesinger Heinrich von Morungen, on entering the order of canons, bequeathed the church a relic of St. Thomas. The choir was changed to Gothic style in 1355. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> was discovered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a> brought wealth to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, the Romanesque nave was demolished and replaced between 1482 and 1496 by the present hall church, which Thilo of Trotha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>, consecrated on 10 April 1496.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The church mainly keeps the character of a late-Gothic hall church, although it was rebuilt over the centuries. It is 76 metres long, its nave 25 metres wide, and its unusually steep roof, pitched at 63 degrees, rises to 45 metres. The tower, first built in 1537 and rebuilt in 1702, is 68 metres high.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> furnishings Bach knew were mostly removed in a Gothic Revival renovation between 1884 and 1889, which also gave the pulpit and the west portal. The bell Gloriosa, cast in 1477 by Theodericus Reinhard, is the largest and is rung only on solemnities. The church has two organs, a Romantic instrument by Wilhelm Sauer, built from 1884 to 1889, and a Bach organ by Gerald Woehl, built from 1999 to 2000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the church became Lutheran, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> preached there on Pentecost Sunday 1539. Mozart played its organ in 1789, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a> was baptised there on 16 August 1813. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> in 1813 the church served as a military hospital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a>, Thomaskantor from 1723, was first buried in the cemetery of the Johanniskirche.</p><p>His bones were identified in 1894 by the anatomist Wilhelm His as very probably his, and after the Johanniskirche burned in the raid of 4 December 1943 they were found undamaged in 1949. They were placed in a new grave in the sanctuary of the Thomaskirche on 28 July 1950, the 200th anniversary of his death. The church was listed in the 2000 World Monuments Watch, which brought funds for repairs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St. Thomas Church, Leipzig” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
